## Facts & Assumptions

**Given:** The Borel probability prior that is uniform on $[0,1]$, the binary sampling rule with success probability $\theta^2(1-\theta)$, and observation $Y=1$.

[F1] The posterior at a fixed datum is the likelihood-weighted prior divided by its evidence when the evidence is positive and finite. [[def-ip-misfit-evidence-and-posterior-law]]

[F2] A point is a strong small-ball MAP when its posterior ball probability divided by the supremum over all centers tends to $1$. [[def-ip-small-ball-map-estimator]]

[F3] On $\mathbb R$, Lebesgue measure assigns an interval its length and integrates the displayed polynomial by its antiderivative. [[def-lebesgue-measure-and-the-lebesgue-sigma-algebra]]

## Verification

1.1 On $[0,1]$, $p(\theta)=\theta^2(1-\theta)\ge0$. Its derivative is $p'(\theta)=\theta(2-3\theta)$, so its maximum is $p(2/3)=4/27<1$. Therefore $p$ and $1-p$ define valid conditional probabilities for $Y=1$ and $Y=0$. By [F3], the evidence for $Y=1$ is
$$ Z=\int_0^1\theta^2(1-\theta)\,d\theta=\frac13-\frac14=\frac1{12}>0. $$
The posterior formula [F1] gives density $q(\theta)=12\theta^2(1-\theta)$ on $[0,1]$, extended by zero on $\mathbb R\setminus[0,1]$. [F1, F3, given, algebra]

2.1 On $(0,1)$, $q'(\theta)=12\theta(2-3\theta)$, so $q$ increases up to $2/3$ and decreases after it; $q(0)=q(1)=0$. Thus $q_{\max}=q(2/3)>0$ is the unique density maximum. For any center $x\in\mathbb R$ and $r>0$, [F3] gives $m_r(x)\le2r q_{\max}$. For $0<r<1/3$, the interval $[2/3-r,2/3+r]$ lies inside $[0,1]$, and continuity of $q$ gives $m_r(2/3)/(2r)\to q_{\max}$. Hence
$$ 1\ge\frac{m_r(2/3)}{\sup_{x\in\mathbb R}m_r(x)} \ge\frac{m_r(2/3)}{2r q_{\max}}\longrightarrow1. $$
By [F2], $2/3$ is a strong small-ball MAP. [F2, F3, step 1.1, given, algebra]

3.1 The posterior mean is
$$ \int_0^1\theta q(\theta)\,d\theta =12\int_0^1(\theta^3-\theta^4)\,d\theta =12\left(\frac14-\frac15\right)=\frac35. $$
Since $3/5\ne2/3$, the posterior mean differs from the strong small-ball MAP. [F3, step 1.1, step 2.1, given, algebra] ∎
